// Field Guide cards first taught in the 4 camp (Camp I): functions + data.
// Every output below was checked by running it with Node. Node prints arrays and
// objects as [ 'rope', 'axe' ] and { name: 'Ana' }; that is the format shown here.
export const GUIDE_CAMP4 = {
  // ─────────────── Functions ───────────────
  function: {
    term: 'Function', short: 'A named, reusable block of code. Write the steps once, then run them whenever you call the name.',
    what: 'A function is a named group of steps in your program. You give it a name and a list of steps. Later, any time you write its name followed by brackets, like `packBag()`, all of those steps run.\n\nThink of a recipe card. Writing the card doesn\'t cook anything. Cooking happens each time someone picks the card up and follows it. A function works the same way: **defining** it writes the card, **calling** it does the cooking.',
    how: 'The classic way to write one is a **function declaration**:\n```\nfunction greet(name) {\n  return `Welcome, ${name}`\n}\n```\n• `function` is the keyword that says "a function starts here"\n• `greet` is its name (same naming rules as a variable: letters, digits, `_` and `$`, no spaces, can\'t start with a digit)\n• `(name)` lists its **parameters**: the inputs it expects (it can have none: `()`)\n• the curly braces `{ }` hold the **body**: the steps that run on each call\n• `return` hands a result back to whoever called it\n\n**Calling** it means writing the name plus brackets: `greet("Ana")`. The brackets are what make it run. Without them, `greet` only refers to the function. It does not run it.\n\nWhen a call happens, JavaScript jumps into the body, runs it top to bottom, then jumps back to exactly where the call was, carrying the returned value with it.\n\nA quirk worth knowing: JavaScript reads `function` declarations before running anything (this is called **hoisting**), so you can call a declared function on a line above where it\'s written. Arrow functions (a later card) don\'t get this treatment.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'function cheer() {\n  console.log("Keep climbing!")\n}\n\ncheer()\ncheer()', out: 'Keep climbing!\nKeep climbing!', note: 'Defined once, called twice, so it runs twice.' },
      { code: 'function cheer() {\n  console.log("Keep climbing!")\n}', out: '(nothing)', note: 'Only defined, never called. Nothing runs.' },
      { code: 'function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\nconsole.log(add(2, 3))\nconsole.log(add(10, 20))', out: '5\n30', note: 'Same steps, different inputs, different results.' },
      { code: 'function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\nconsole.log(add)', out: '[Function: add]', note: 'No brackets means no call. Node just describes the function itself.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Avoiding copy-paste: when the same steps appear twice, they become one function called twice.', 'Giving a chunk of code a clear name, so `totalWeight(pack)` reads like English.', 'Splitting a big program into small pieces you can test one at a time.', 'Every real app is built from functions: a "Buy" button runs one, a web server runs one per page request.'],
    when: ['You\'re about to copy and paste the same lines a second time.', 'A chunk of code does one clear job you could name, like "work out the total weight."', 'You want to run the same steps with different inputs.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t wrap a single line you only use once in its own function just because you can. It adds a jump for the reader without adding clarity.', 'Don\'t make one giant function that does five jobs. If you need "and" to describe it ("loads the list AND sorts it AND prints it"), split it.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'function cheer() { console.log("Go!") }\n// ...and never calls it', result: '(nothing prints)', why: 'Defining a function only writes the recipe. You must call it: `cheer()`.' },
      { wrong: 'cheer', result: '(nothing happens)', why: 'Without brackets you only mention the function; you don\'t run it. Write `cheer()`.' },
      { wrong: 'Cheer()', result: 'ReferenceError: Cheer is not defined', why: 'Names are case-sensitive. The function is `cheer`, lowercase.' },
      { wrong: 'cheer() { console.log("Go!") }', result: 'SyntaxError: Unexpected token \'{\'', why: 'You left out the `function` keyword, so JavaScript read `cheer()` as a call and then got confused by the `{`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Name functions with a verb that says what they do: `addItem`, `totalWeight`, `isPacked`. Then a reader knows what a call does without opening it.', 'One function, one job. Small functions are easy to test and hard to break.', 'Stuck on a bug inside a function? Call it on its own with simple inputs, like `add(2, 3)`, and check the answer.'],
    related: ['parameter', 'return-value', 'arrow-function', 'scope'],
  },
  parameter: {
    term: 'Parameters and arguments', short: 'Parameters are a function\'s named inputs. Arguments are the actual values you pass in when you call it.',
    what: 'A **parameter** is a placeholder name in a function\'s definition, like a blank on a form. An **argument** is the real value you fill that blank with when you call the function.\n\nIn `function campHeight(base, climbed)`, `base` and `climbed` are parameters. In the call `campHeight(5364, 600)`, `5364` and `600` are the arguments.',
    how: 'When you call a function, JavaScript matches arguments to parameters **by position**, left to right: the first argument goes into the first parameter, the second into the second, and so on. The names don\'t matter for the matching, only the order.\n\nInside the body, each parameter behaves like a variable holding the value that came in. Every call fills them fresh.\n\nWhat if the counts don\'t match?\n• **Too few arguments:** the missing parameters are `undefined`. Maths with undefined gives `NaN` ("not a number").\n• **Too many arguments:** the extras are silently ignored.\n• **Default values:** write `name = "climber"` in the brackets, and that value is used whenever the argument is left out.\n\nArguments can be any value: numbers, strings, booleans, arrays, objects, even other functions (you\'ll see that with `map` and `filter`).',
    usage: [
      { code: 'function campHeight(base, climbed) {\n  return base + climbed\n}\nconsole.log(campHeight(5364, 600))', out: '5964', note: 'base gets 5364, climbed gets 600: matched by position.' },
      { code: 'function describe(item, kg) {\n  return `${item} weighs ${kg} kg`\n}\nconsole.log(describe("tent", 3))\nconsole.log(describe(3, "tent"))', out: 'tent weighs 3 kg\n3 weighs tent kg', note: 'Swap the order and the values land in the wrong slots.' },
      { code: 'function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\nconsole.log(add(2))', out: 'NaN', note: 'b was never given, so it\'s undefined, and 2 + undefined is NaN.' },
      { code: 'function greet(name = "climber") {\n  return `Hi ${name}`\n}\nconsole.log(greet())\nconsole.log(greet("Ana"))', out: 'Hi climber\nHi Ana', note: 'A default parameter fills in when the argument is missing.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Making one function work for many cases: `totalWeight(myPack)`, `totalWeight(yourPack)`.', 'Passing settings in, like `formatHeight(meters, "feet")`.', 'Handing a function to another function (a callback), like the `n => n * 2` you give to `map`.'],
    when: ['The function needs information from outside to do its job.', 'You notice two functions that are identical except for one value. Make that value a parameter and keep one function.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t pile on parameters. Past about three, calls like `plan(3, true, "x", 9, false)` become unreadable. Pass one object instead: `plan({ days: 3, guided: true })`.', 'Don\'t reach for an outside variable when the value could simply be passed in. Inputs through parameters make a function predictable.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'describe(3, "tent")   // meant describe("tent", 3)', result: '3 weighs tent kg', why: 'Arguments match by position, not by meaning. Check the order in the definition.' },
      { wrong: 'add(2)', result: 'NaN', why: 'The second parameter was undefined. Pass every argument, or give the parameter a default.' },
      { wrong: 'function greet("name") { return "Hi" }', result: 'SyntaxError: Unexpected string', why: 'Parameters are names, not strings. Write `function greet(name)` without quotes. The quotes go on the argument in the call: `greet("Ana")`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Say it out loud: "parameters are the blanks, arguments are what I write in them."', 'If a function behaves oddly, `console.log` its parameters on the first line of the body to see what actually came in.'],
    related: ['function', 'return-value', 'scope'],
  },
  'return-value': {
    term: 'return', short: 'Sends a value back out of a function to where it was called, and ends the function right there.',
    what: '`return` is how a function hands its answer back. The call itself then **becomes** that value, so you can store it, print it, or feed it into something else.\n\nPrinting with `console.log` shows a value to a person. Returning gives it to the program, which can store it or pass it on.',
    how: '• `return something` works out `something`, ends the function immediately, and sends the value back to the caller.\n• Any lines after a `return` in the same block never run.\n• A function with **no** `return` (or a bare `return;`) gives back `undefined`.\n• You can return any value: a number, a string, a boolean, an array, an object.\n\n**`return` vs `console.log`:** `console.log` only shows a value on the screen for a human. The program can\'t use what was printed. `return` gives the value to the program. A function can do both, but if other code needs the answer, it must `return` it.\n\n**One trap:** keep the value on the same line as `return`. JavaScript treats a line ending right after `return` as `return;` and gives back undefined.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'function double(n) {\n  return n * 2\n}\nconst x = double(4)\nconsole.log(x + 1)', out: '9', note: 'double(4) becomes 8, so x is 8, then 8 + 1.' },
      { code: 'function double(n) {\n  console.log(n * 2)\n}\nconst x = double(4)\nconsole.log(x)', out: '8\nundefined', note: 'It printed 8 but returned nothing, so x is undefined.' },
      { code: 'function check(kg) {\n  return "checked"\n  console.log("never runs")\n}\nconsole.log(check(3))', out: 'checked', note: 'return ends the function. The line after it is dead code.' },
      { code: 'function weights() {\n  return [3, 4, 1]\n}\nconsole.log(weights().length)', out: '3', note: 'You can return an array (or anything) and use it straight away.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Calculations whose result is used elsewhere: `const total = totalWeight(pack)`.', 'Yes/no checks: `function isHeavy(kg) { return kg > 20 }`, used inside an if.', 'Building values step by step: one function returns a list, another filters it, a third prints it.'],
    when: ['Any time the caller needs the result for something else.', 'To stop early once you know the answer, for example returning as soon as you find the item you were searching for.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t use console.log where a return belongs. Printing is for people; returning is for code.', 'Don\'t put a line break straight after `return`. `return` followed by the value on the next line returns undefined.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'function add(a, b) {\n  a + b\n}\nconsole.log(add(2, 3))', result: 'undefined', why: 'The sum was worked out and thrown away. Write `return a + b`.' },
      { wrong: 'function peak() {\n  return\n    "summit"\n}\nconsole.log(peak())', result: 'undefined', why: 'A line ending after `return` counts as `return;`. Keep the value on the same line: `return "summit"`.' },
      { prose: 'Logging inside a function, then wondering why the variable you stored the call in is undefined.', why: 'Printing isn\'t returning. If you want to use the value, return it, and print it outside if you also want to see it.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Good shape for most functions: take inputs in through parameters, give one answer out through return, and don\'t print inside. Print the result where you call it.', 'If you ever see `undefined` where you expected a value, the first suspect is a missing `return`.'],
    related: ['function', 'parameter', 'console-log'],
  },
  'arrow-function': {
    term: 'Arrow function', short: 'A shorter way to write a function: (inputs) => result.',
    what: 'An arrow function is a compact way to write a function, named after the `=>` arrow in the middle. It\'s usually stored in a `const`:\n`const double = n => n * 2`\nThis means the same as `function double(n) { return n * 2 }`.\n\nYou\'ll see arrow functions everywhere in modern JavaScript, especially as the little helper functions you hand to `map` and `filter`.',
    how: 'The parts: `(parameters) => body`.\n\n**Brackets around the parameters:**\n• none: `() => "ready"`\n• one: the brackets are optional, so `n => n * 2` and `(n) => n * 2` are the same\n• two or more: required, as in `(a, b) => a + b`\n\n**The body comes in two styles:**\n• **Short (expression) body**, no braces: the value is **returned automatically**. `(a, b) => a + b` returns the sum.\n• **Block body**, with braces: works like a normal function body, so you must write `return` yourself. `(a, b) => { return a + b }`.\n\nTo return an **object** from a short body, wrap it in round brackets: `() => ({ name: "Ana" })`. Without them, JavaScript reads the `{` as the start of a block body.\n\n**Order matters:** an arrow function stored in a `const` exists only from that line on. Calling it on an earlier line throws an error (unlike a `function` declaration).\n\nArrow functions also handle a special keyword called `this` differently from regular functions. That matters for functions stored inside objects, which you\'ll meet later; until then, the two styles do the same job.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const double = n => n * 2\nconsole.log(double(21))', out: '42', note: 'One parameter, short body, automatic return.' },
      { code: 'const add = (a, b) => a + b\nconsole.log(add(2, 3))', out: '5', note: 'Two parameters need brackets.' },
      { code: 'const greet = () => "Namaste"\nconsole.log(greet())', out: 'Namaste', note: 'No parameters: empty brackets.' },
      { code: 'const add = (a, b) => {\n  const sum = a + b\n  return sum\n}\nconsole.log(add(2, 3))', out: '5', note: 'Block body with braces: you write return yourself.' },
      { code: 'const makeTent = () => ({ name: "Storm Dome", kg: 3 })\nconsole.log(makeTent().kg)', out: '3', note: 'Round brackets around the object so it gets returned.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Short helper functions handed to `map`, `filter` and `find`: `gear.filter(item => item.kg > 5)`.', 'Quick one-line calculations: `const toFeet = m => m * 3.28`.', 'Event handlers in web pages and React components, later in the climb.'],
    when: ['The function is short, especially a one-liner.', 'You\'re passing a function into another function, like `map(c => c.name)`.'],
    whenNot: ['When the logic is long and important. A named `function` declaration can be easier to find and read.', 'As a function stored inside an object that uses `this` (covered later). Arrow functions don\'t get their own `this`, which surprises people.', 'Don\'t call it above the line where it\'s defined. It doesn\'t exist yet.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'const add = (a, b) => { a + b }\nconsole.log(add(2, 3))', result: 'undefined', why: 'Braces turn on block-body mode, which needs `return`. Either drop the braces (`(a, b) => a + b`) or write `{ return a + b }`.' },
      { wrong: 'const tent = () => { name: "Storm Dome" }\nconsole.log(tent())', result: 'undefined', why: 'The `{` was read as a block body, not an object. Wrap it: `() => ({ name: "Storm Dome" })`.' },
      { wrong: 'console.log(half(10))\nconst half = n => n / 2', result: 'ReferenceError: Cannot access \'half\' before initialization', why: 'A const-stored arrow function only exists from its own line onwards. Define it first, call it after.' },
      { wrong: 'const add = a, b => a + b', result: 'SyntaxError: Missing initializer in const declaration', why: 'Without brackets, the comma splits the line into two declarations (`add = a`, then `b`), and every const needs a value. Two or more parameters need brackets: `(a, b) => a + b`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Read `=>` out loud as "goes to" or "becomes": `n => n * 2` is "n becomes n times two."', 'Start with the short body. Only add braces when you need more than one line, and remember the `return` when you do.'],
    related: ['function', 'return-value', 'map-filter'],
  },
  scope: {
    term: 'Scope', short: 'Where in your code a variable can be seen. Variables made inside a function or block stay inside it.',
    what: 'Scope is the part of your code where a variable can be used. A variable made with `let` or `const` inside a pair of curly braces (a function body, an if block, a loop) only exists inside those braces. Code outside can\'t see it.\n\nPicture tents at a camp. Whatever you keep inside your tent is yours; people outside can\'t reach in. But from inside your tent you can still see the whole camp around you.',
    how: 'The rules, from the outside in:\n• **Top-level (global) scope:** variables declared outside every function and block can be read from anywhere in the file.\n• **Function scope:** parameters and variables declared inside a function belong to that function. Each **call** gets a brand-new set, which disappears when the call ends.\n• **Block scope:** `let` and `const` inside any `{ }`, such as an if or a loop, only live inside that block.\n• **Inner sees outer, outer can\'t see inner:** code inside a function can read variables from the scopes around it. Code outside can\'t reach into a function.\n• **Shadowing:** if an inner variable has the same name as an outer one, the inner one wins inside its block, and the outer one is untouched.\n\nWhen JavaScript meets a name, it looks in the current scope first, then the one around it, and so on outwards. If it reaches the top without finding it, you get `ReferenceError: … is not defined`.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'function packCount() {\n  const items = 12\n  return items\n}\nconsole.log(packCount())\nconsole.log(items)', out: '12\nReferenceError: items is not defined', note: 'items lives inside the function. Outside, it doesn\'t exist.' },
      { code: 'const peak = "Everest"\nfunction show() {\n  return `Climbing ${peak}`\n}\nconsole.log(show())', out: 'Climbing Everest', note: 'Inner code can read an outer variable.' },
      { code: 'function tick() {\n  let n = 0\n  n = n + 1\n  return n\n}\nconsole.log(tick())\nconsole.log(tick())', out: '1\n1', note: 'Every call starts with a fresh n. Nothing carries over between calls.' },
      { code: 'const camp = "Base"\nfunction move() {\n  const camp = "Camp I"\n  return camp\n}\nconsole.log(move())\nconsole.log(camp)', out: 'Camp I\nBase', note: 'Shadowing: the inner camp hides the outer one inside the function only.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Keeping helper variables private to the function that needs them, so they can\'t clash with names elsewhere.', 'Letting two functions both use a variable called `total` without interfering.', 'Understanding "is not defined" errors, which are very often scope problems.'],
    when: ['Always. Every variable has a scope. The habit is to declare each variable in the smallest scope that needs it.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t make everything global "so it\'s reachable." Global variables can be changed from anywhere, which makes bugs hard to track down.', 'Don\'t lean on a function quietly changing an outer variable. Pass values in as parameters and send results out with return.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'function addUp() {\n  const total = 5 + 7\n}\naddUp()\nconsole.log(total)', result: 'ReferenceError: total is not defined', why: 'total was local to addUp. Return it instead: `return 5 + 7`, then `const total = addUp()`.' },
      { wrong: 'if (true) {\n  const msg = "inside"\n}\nconsole.log(msg)', result: 'ReferenceError: msg is not defined', why: 'const and let are block-scoped. Declare msg before the if if you need it after.' },
      { prose: 'Expecting a local variable to remember its value between calls.', why: 'Each call gets fresh local variables. If something must persist, keep it outside the function or pass it in and return it.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Declare variables as close as possible to where they\'re used.', 'Seeing "is not defined"? Ask: where was it declared, and am I standing inside those braces right now?', 'Avoid reusing outer names inside functions (shadowing) while you\'re learning. It\'s legal but confusing.'],
    related: ['function', 'parameter', 'return-value'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Lists (arrays) ───────────────
  array: {
    term: 'Array', short: 'An ordered list of values in square brackets: ["rope", "axe", "tent"].',
    what: 'An array is a list that keeps its items in order, all stored under one name. Instead of `gear1`, `gear2`, `gear3`, you have one `gear` list holding everything.\n\nLike a packing list on a clipboard: one sheet, items numbered from the top, and you can add or cross off lines.',
    how: 'Write an array with square brackets and commas between items:\n`const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]`\n\n• Items can be any type, mixed if you like: `[1, "two", true]`. Usually a list holds one kind of thing.\n• `[]` is an **empty array**.\n• `.length` tells you how many items there are. It\'s a property, not a function, so no brackets: `gear.length`.\n• Each item has a position number called an **index**, starting at 0 (see the next card).\n\n**const and arrays:** `const gear = [...]` stops you pointing `gear` at a different list, but you can still change what\'s *inside* it (add, remove, replace items).\n\n**Arrays are shared, not copied:** `const b = a` doesn\'t make a second list. Both names point to the same one, so changing `b` changes `a` too. The same happens when you pass an array into a function.\n\nNode prints arrays like this: `[ \'rope\', \'axe\' ]`. Browser consoles show the same array slightly differently, such as `["rope", "axe"]`. Same list, different display.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]\nconsole.log(gear)', out: '[ \'rope\', \'ice axe\', \'tent\' ]', note: 'How Node displays an array.' },
      { code: 'const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]\nconsole.log(gear.length)', out: '3', note: 'How many items. No brackets after length.' },
      { code: 'const empty = []\nconsole.log(empty.length)', out: '0' },
      { code: 'const a = ["rope"]\nconst b = a\nb.push("axe")\nconsole.log(a)', out: '[ \'rope\', \'axe\' ]', note: 'a and b are the same list, so a changed too.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Any collection: a packing list, the climbers on a team, the scores on a leaderboard, the lines of a file.', 'Results from searches and databases, which almost always come back as arrays.', 'Anything you want to loop over, count, sort, or filter.'],
    when: ['You have several values of the same kind, and their order matters or might.', 'You don\'t know in advance how many items there will be.'],
    whenNot: ['When each value has a different meaning, like a climber\'s name, age and camp. `["Ana", 29, 1]` forces everyone to remember that position 1 is the age. Use an object: `{ name: "Ana", age: 29, camp: 1 }`.', 'When you need to look things up by a name rather than a position. Objects are built for that.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'const gear = ["rope" "axe"]', result: 'SyntaxError: Unexpected string', why: 'Items need commas between them: `["rope", "axe"]`.' },
      { wrong: 'gear.length()', result: 'TypeError: gear.length is not a function', why: 'length is a property, not a function. Write `gear.length`.' },
      { prose: 'Writing `const backup = gear` and expecting a separate copy.', why: 'Both names point at the same array. To copy it, use `const backup = [...gear]` (the `...` spreads the items into a new list).' },
    ],
    tips: ['Name arrays in the plural (`climbers`, `items`) and single values in the singular (`climber`). Then `for (const climber of climbers)` reads naturally.', '`typeof []` says "object" (arrays are a kind of object). To check for an array, use `Array.isArray(x)`, which gives true or false.'],
    related: ['array-index', 'array-methods', 'for-of', 'object'],
  },
  'array-index': {
    term: 'Array index', short: 'An item\'s position number. Counting starts at 0, so the first item is list[0].',
    what: 'Every item in an array has an **index**: its position number. JavaScript counts from **zero**, so the first item is index 0, the second is index 1, and so on. You reach an item with square brackets: `gear[0]`.\n\nIt works like floor numbers in many countries: the ground floor is 0, the floor above is 1.',
    how: 'For `const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]`:\n• `gear[0]` is "rope", `gear[1]` is "ice axe", `gear[2]` is "tent"\n• the **last** index is always `length - 1`, so `gear[gear.length - 1]` is the last item whatever the length\n• `gear.at(-1)` also gives the last item (`at` accepts negative numbers that count from the end)\n• asking for an index that doesn\'t exist, like `gear[5]` or `gear[-1]`, gives `undefined`. No error, just nothing there.\n\nYou can also **replace** an item by assigning to its index: `gear[1] = "trekking pole"`.\n\nThe brackets can hold any expression that works out to a number: `gear[i]`, `gear[i + 1]`, `gear[gear.length - 1]`.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]\nconsole.log(gear[0])\nconsole.log(gear[2])', out: 'rope\ntent', note: 'First item is index 0.' },
      { code: 'console.log(gear[gear.length - 1])\nconsole.log(gear.at(-1))', out: 'tent\ntent', note: 'Two ways to get the last item.' },
      { code: 'console.log(gear[3])\nconsole.log(gear[-1])', out: 'undefined\nundefined', note: 'Out of range: undefined, not an error.' },
      { code: 'gear[1] = "trekking pole"\nconsole.log(gear)', out: '[ \'rope\', \'trekking pole\', \'tent\' ]', note: 'Replace an item by its index.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Grabbing the first or last entry: the newest message, the top score.', 'Stepping through a list by position in a classic `for` loop: `gear[i]`.', 'Replacing a specific item in place.'],
    when: ['You know the position you want.', 'You need the first or last item.'],
    whenNot: ['When you\'re searching for an item by its value. Use `indexOf`, `includes` or `find` instead of guessing positions.', 'When positions might shift. After a removal, every later item moves down one, so a remembered index can point at the wrong thing.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'gear[gear.length]', result: 'undefined', why: 'Off by one. With 3 items the indexes are 0, 1, 2, so the last is `gear[gear.length - 1]`.' },
      { wrong: 'gear[1]   // expecting the first item', result: 'ice axe', why: 'Counting starts at 0. The first item is `gear[0]`.' },
      { wrong: 'const team = [{ name: "Ana" }]\nconsole.log(team[1].name)', result: 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \'name\')', why: 'team[1] doesn\'t exist, so it\'s undefined, and undefined has no properties. Check the index (and the length) first.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Say "item zero, item one" out loud while learning. It helps you get used to counting from 0.', 'Seeing "Cannot read properties of undefined"? Very often an index went past the end of a list.'],
    related: ['array', 'array-methods', 'nested-data'],
  },
  'array-methods': {
    term: 'Array methods', short: 'Built-in tools every array carries: push, pop, includes, indexOf, join, slice and more.',
    what: 'A **method** is a function that belongs to a value. Every array comes with a toolkit of methods, used with a dot: `gear.push("rope")`. They add, remove, search and combine items so you don\'t have to write those loops yourself.',
    how: 'The everyday ones:\n• `push(x)` adds x to the **end**. Returns the new length.\n• `pop()` removes the **last** item and returns it.\n• `unshift(x)` adds to the **start**; `shift()` removes the first item and returns it.\n• `includes(x)` gives true or false: is x in the list?\n• `indexOf(x)` gives the index of the first x, or **-1** if it isn\'t there.\n• `join(sep)` glues all items into one string with sep between them (a comma if you leave it out).\n• `slice(start, end)` returns a **new** array: a copy of the items from start up to, but not including, end.\n• `splice(start, count)` removes count items starting at start, **from the original array**, and returns the removed items.\n\n**Changes the original, or makes a new one?** This matters a lot.\n• **Change the array itself:** push, pop, shift, unshift, splice, sort.\n• **Leave it alone and return something new:** includes, indexOf, join, slice (and map and filter, on a later card).\n\n`includes` and `indexOf` don\'t convert types when they compare, so the number `3` and the string `"3"` don\'t match.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const gear = ["rope", "ice axe"]\ngear.push("stove")\nconsole.log(gear.length)\nconsole.log(gear.pop())\nconsole.log(gear)', out: '3\nstove\n[ \'rope\', \'ice axe\' ]', note: 'push adds to the end; pop takes the last item off and hands it to you.' },
      { code: 'console.log(gear.includes("rope"))\nconsole.log(gear.indexOf("ice axe"))\nconsole.log(gear.indexOf("tent"))', out: 'true\n1\n-1', note: 'Searching. -1 means "not found."' },
      { code: 'console.log(["Base", "Camp I", "Camp II"].join(" > "))', out: 'Base > Camp I > Camp II', note: 'One string from a list.' },
      { code: 'const camps = ["Base", "I", "II", "III"]\nconsole.log(camps.slice(1, 3))\nconsole.log(camps)', out: '[ \'I\', \'II\' ]\n[ \'Base\', \'I\', \'II\', \'III\' ]', note: 'slice copies a section and leaves the original alone.' },
      { code: 'const pack = ["rope", "axe", "tent"]\npack.splice(1, 1)\nconsole.log(pack)', out: '[ \'rope\', \'tent\' ]', note: 'splice removes from the original: 1 item, starting at index 1.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Building a list as you go: start with `[]` and `push` each new item.', 'Checking membership: `if (gear.includes("rope"))`.', 'Turning a list into a sentence for the screen: `names.join(", ")`.', 'Taking the top few results with `slice(0, 3)`.'],
    when: ['You need to add, remove, search or combine items. There\'s almost always a method for it.', 'Before writing a loop, check whether a method already does the job in one line.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t use a method that changes the original (push, splice, sort) on an array other code is still using, unless that\'s exactly what you want. Copy it first or use a method that returns a new array.', 'Don\'t pass `indexOf`\'s result straight into `splice` without checking for -1. `splice(-1, 1)` removes the **last** item, not nothing.', 'Don\'t sort numbers with plain `sort()`. It sorts them as text.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'let gear = ["rope"]\ngear = gear.push("axe")\nconsole.log(gear)', result: '2', why: 'push changes the array and returns the new length. Assigning that back replaced your list with the number 2. Just write `gear.push("axe")`. (With const you\'d get `TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.`)' },
      { wrong: 'console.log([10, 9, 1].sort())', result: '[ 1, 10, 9 ]', why: 'sort compares items as text by default, and "10" comes before "9" alphabetically. For numbers use `sort((a, b) => a - b)`, which gives `[ 1, 9, 10 ]`.' },
      { wrong: 'const pack = ["rope", "axe", "stove"]\npack.splice(pack.indexOf("tent"), 1)\nconsole.log(pack)', result: '[ \'rope\', \'axe\' ]', why: '"tent" wasn\'t there, so indexOf gave -1, and splice(-1, 1) removed the last item. Check `indexOf(...) !== -1` first.' },
      { wrong: '[3, 1, 10].includes("3")', result: 'false', why: 'No type conversion: the string "3" is not the number 3.' },
    ],
    tips: ['When you meet a new method, ask two questions: what does it **return**, and does it **change the original**?', '`console.log` the array right after calling a method while learning. You\'ll see exactly what it did.'],
    related: ['array', 'array-index', 'for-of', 'map-filter'],
  },
  'for-of': {
    term: 'for...of loop', short: 'Runs a block once for every item in a list, handing you each item in turn.',
    what: '`for...of` walks through an array from first item to last and runs the same block for each one. You don\'t deal with index numbers or lengths at all; you just get the items.\n\nLike checking gear at the trailhead: pick up each item, check it, put it down, pick up the next, until the pile is done.',
    how: '```\nfor (const item of gear) {\n  console.log(item)\n}\n```\n• `item` is a new variable that holds the current item. You choose the name.\n• It uses `const` because each round gets a fresh variable; you\'re not changing it inside the loop.\n• `of gear` says which list to walk through.\n• The block runs once per item, in order, and the loop ends after the last one.\n\nIt works on anything **iterable** (walkable item by item): arrays, strings (letter by letter), and some others you\'ll meet later. Plain objects are **not** iterable; to walk an object, loop over `Object.keys(obj)` or `Object.entries(obj)`.\n\nNeed the position too? Use `for (const [i, item] of gear.entries())`, which hands you the index and the item each round. Or use a classic counting `for` loop.\n\nYou can still use `break` to stop early and `continue` to skip to the next item, just as with other loops.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const gear = ["rope", "ice axe", "tent"]\nfor (const item of gear) {\n  console.log(`Packed: ${item}`)\n}', out: 'Packed: rope\nPacked: ice axe\nPacked: tent' },
      { code: 'let total = 0\nfor (const kg of [3, 4, 1]) {\n  total = total + kg\n}\nconsole.log(total)', out: '8', note: 'The classic "add everything up" pattern.' },
      { code: 'for (const [i, item] of ["rope", "axe"].entries()) {\n  console.log(i, item)\n}', out: '0 rope\n1 axe', note: 'When you need the index as well.' },
      { code: 'for (const letter of "axe") {\n  console.log(letter)\n}', out: 'a\nx\ne', note: 'Strings are iterable too.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Doing something with every item: printing a list, adding up weights, checking every climber.', 'Searching a list and stopping with `break` (or `return` inside a function) once you find what you need.', 'Building a new value from a list, like a total or a count.'],
    when: ['You want each item and don\'t care about its position.', 'Most loops over arrays. It\'s the clearest, hardest-to-get-wrong way to visit every item.'],
    whenNot: ['When you need to count or jump by positions (every second item, backwards). Use a classic `for (let i = 0; …)` loop.', 'On a plain object: it throws "is not iterable". Use `Object.keys(obj)` or `Object.entries(obj)`.', 'Don\'t confuse it with `for...in`, which walks **keys**. On an array it gives the indexes as strings ("0", "1"), not the items.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'const climber = { name: "Ana", camp: 1 }\nfor (const x of climber) {}', result: 'TypeError: climber is not iterable', why: 'Objects can\'t be walked with for...of. Use `for (const key of Object.keys(climber))`.' },
      { wrong: 'for (const x in ["rope", "axe"]) {\n  console.log(x)\n}', result: '0\n1', why: '`in` walks keys (the indexes, as strings). You wanted `of`, which walks the items.' },
      { wrong: 'for (let item of gear) {\n  item = "X"\n}', result: '(gear is unchanged)', why: 'item is its own variable holding what was in the slot, not a link to the slot itself, so assigning to it doesn\'t touch the list. To replace items, use the index (`gear[i] = …`) or build a new list with map.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Name the loop variable as the singular of the list: `for (const climber of climbers)`. Reads like a sentence.', 'Default to for...of for arrays. Reach for the counting `for` loop only when you truly need the numbers.'],
    related: ['array', 'array-methods', 'map-filter', 'object'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Objects and nested data ───────────────
  object: {
    term: 'Object', short: 'A bundle of named values: { name: "Ana", camp: 1 }. Look things up by label, not position.',
    what: 'An object groups related values together, each with its own **name** (called a **key** or **property name**). Where an array is a numbered list, an object is a labelled form: name, camp, oxygen, each in its own box.\n\n`const climber = { name: "Ana", camp: 1, oxygen: true }`',
    how: 'Write an object with curly braces. Inside, each entry is `key: value`, separated by commas:\n```\nconst climber = {\n  name: "Ana",\n  camp: 1,\n  oxygen: true,\n}\n```\n• Keys are names (usually written without quotes). Values can be anything: numbers, strings, booleans, arrays, other objects, even functions.\n• A comma after the last entry is allowed, and handy when you add lines later.\n• `{}` is an empty object.\n• Order isn\'t how you find things. You find a value by its key (see the property access card).\n\n**const and objects:** like arrays, `const` stops you replacing the whole object, but you can still change, add and remove its properties.\n\n**Objects are shared, not copied:** `const b = a` gives two names for one object. And `===` on objects checks "is this the very same object?", not "do they look alike?", so `{ a: 1 } === { a: 1 }` is false.\n\nHandy helpers: `Object.keys(obj)` gives an array of the keys, `Object.values(obj)` the values, `Object.entries(obj)` the `[key, value]` pairs.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const climber = { name: "Ana", camp: 1, oxygen: true }\nconsole.log(climber)', out: '{ name: \'Ana\', camp: 1, oxygen: true }', note: 'How Node displays an object.' },
      { code: 'console.log(climber.name)', out: 'Ana', note: 'Read one value by its key.' },
      { code: 'console.log(Object.keys(climber))', out: '[ \'name\', \'camp\', \'oxygen\' ]' },
      { code: 'console.log({ a: 1 } === { a: 1 })', out: 'false', note: 'Two separate objects, even if they look the same.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Describing one thing with several facts: a climber, a piece of gear, a user account, a product.', 'Settings: `{ units: "metric", darkMode: true }`.', 'Data from the internet (APIs) arrives as objects, usually in a format called JSON that you\'ll meet in Camp II.'],
    when: ['The values belong together and each has its own meaning.', 'You want to look something up by a name instead of a position.'],
    whenNot: ['For a list of similar things in order. Use an array (often an array of objects).', 'Don\'t compare two objects with `===` to see if they hold the same data. It only checks whether they\'re the very same object. Compare the properties you care about instead.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'const c = { name = "Ana" }', result: 'SyntaxError: Invalid shorthand property initializer', why: 'Inside an object, keys and values are joined with a colon, not `=`. Write `{ name: "Ana" }`.' },
      { wrong: 'const c = { name: "Ana" camp: 1 }', result: 'SyntaxError: Unexpected identifier \'camp\'', why: 'Entries need commas between them: `{ name: "Ana", camp: 1 }`.' },
      { wrong: 'climber.map(x => x)', result: 'TypeError: climber.map is not a function', why: 'map is an array method. An object isn\'t an array, so it doesn\'t have it.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Put one property per line when an object has more than two or three. Much easier to read and to change.', 'Choose clear, consistent keys (`kg` everywhere, not `kg` in one place and `weight` in another). Your future self will search for them.'],
    related: ['property-access', 'nested-data', 'array'],
  },
  'property-access': {
    term: 'Property access (dot and brackets)', short: 'Read or change an object\'s values with obj.key or obj["key"].',
    what: 'To get a value out of an object you name its key, in one of two ways:\n• **dot notation:** `climber.name`\n• **bracket notation:** `climber["name"]`\nBoth read the same property. The same syntax also lets you change a value or add a brand-new one.',
    how: '**Dot notation** is the everyday choice: short and readable. It works when the key is a plain name (letters, digits, `_`, `$`, not starting with a digit).\n\n**Bracket notation** takes a string, so you need it when:\n• the key has a space or a dash: `gear["max-weight"]`\n• the key is stored in a variable: `const key = "camp"; climber[key]`\n\n**Changing and adding:** assign to a property. If it exists, it\'s replaced; if not, it\'s created.\n`climber.camp = 2` (change) · `climber.team = "Blue rope"` (add)\n\n**Removing:** `delete climber.team`.\n\n**Missing keys** give `undefined`, with no error. That\'s handy, but it also means a typo like `climber.nmae` fails silently.\n\n**Checking:** `"name" in climber` gives true or false.\n\n**Optional chaining** `?.` stops safely if something on the way is missing: `climber.tent?.brand` gives undefined instead of crashing when there\'s no tent.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const climber = { name: "Ana", camp: 1 }\nconsole.log(climber.name)\nconsole.log(climber["camp"])', out: 'Ana\n1', note: 'Two ways to read.' },
      { code: 'climber.camp = 2\nclimber.team = "Blue rope"\nconsole.log(climber)', out: '{ name: \'Ana\', camp: 2, team: \'Blue rope\' }', note: 'Change one property, add another.' },
      { code: 'const key = "name"\nconsole.log(climber[key])', out: 'Ana', note: 'The key is in a variable, so brackets (no quotes around key).' },
      { code: 'console.log(climber.age)\nconsole.log("age" in climber)', out: 'undefined\nfalse', note: 'Missing property: undefined, no error.' },
      { code: 'const gear = { "max-weight": 20 }\nconsole.log(gear["max-weight"])', out: '20', note: 'A dash in the key, so brackets are required.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Reading fields from data: `order.total`, `user.email`, `item.kg`.', 'Updating state: `climber.camp = climber.camp + 1` when they move up.', 'Looking up a value chosen at runtime: `prices[itemName]`.'],
    when: ['Dot notation: almost always.', 'Brackets: the key is in a variable, or has spaces, dashes or other unusual characters.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t use brackets without quotes when you mean a fixed key. `climber[name]` looks for a **variable** called name.', 'Don\'t assume a missing property will error. It returns undefined, so double-check spelling when a value is mysteriously undefined.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'climber[camp]', result: 'ReferenceError: camp is not defined', why: 'Without quotes, camp is treated as a variable name. Write `climber["camp"]` or `climber.camp`.' },
      { wrong: 'gear.max-weight', result: 'ReferenceError: weight is not defined', why: 'JavaScript reads this as `gear.max` minus `weight`. Use `gear["max-weight"]`.' },
      { wrong: 'climber.nmae', result: 'undefined', why: 'A typo is just a missing property, so there\'s no error to warn you. Check spelling first when you see a surprise undefined.' },
      { wrong: 'const c = {}\nc.tent.brand = "Storm"', result: 'TypeError: Cannot set properties of undefined (setting \'brand\')', why: 'c.tent doesn\'t exist yet. Create it first: `c.tent = { brand: "Storm" }`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Prefer dot notation; switch to brackets only when you must.', 'A surprise undefined is usually a spelling mismatch between where you set a key and where you read it.'],
    related: ['object', 'nested-data', 'array-index'],
  },
  'nested-data': {
    term: 'Nested data', short: 'Arrays and objects inside each other: a list of climbers, each with their own gear list.',
    what: 'Real data is rarely flat. A team is a **list of climbers**; each climber is an **object**; each object might hold a **list of gear**. Putting arrays and objects inside each other is called nesting, and it\'s how almost every app stores and sends its data.',
    how: 'You read nested data by **chaining** steps, left to right, one level at a time:\n```\nconst team = [\n  { name: "Ana", gear: ["rope", "stove"] },\n  { name: "Kai", gear: ["radio"] },\n]\n```\n• `team[0]` is the first climber object\n• `team[0].name` is "Ana"\n• `team[0].gear` is her gear array\n• `team[0].gear[1]` is "stove"\n\nRead it like directions: "the team, item 0, its gear, item 1."\n\nThe most common shape by far is an **array of objects**, a list where every item has the same keys. It\'s exactly what database rows and API responses look like, and it\'s what `map`, `filter` and `for...of` are made for.\n\nIf any step in the chain is missing, the next step crashes: `team[5].name` fails because `team[5]` is undefined. Optional chaining (`team[5]?.name`) returns undefined instead.\n\nWhen Node prints deeply nested data it shortens the deepest levels to `[Array]` or `[Object]`. The data is still there; it\'s only hidden in the display.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'const team = [\n  { name: "Ana", gear: ["rope", "stove"] },\n  { name: "Kai", gear: ["radio"] },\n]\nconsole.log(team[0].name)\nconsole.log(team[0].gear[1])\nconsole.log(team[1].gear.length)', out: 'Ana\nstove\n1', note: 'Chain one step at a time.' },
      { code: 'for (const climber of team) {\n  console.log(`${climber.name}: ${climber.gear.join(", ")}`)\n}', out: 'Ana: rope, stove\nKai: radio', note: 'Loop over the outer list, dig into each item.' },
      { code: 'const expedition = { leader: { name: "Ana", camp: 2 } }\nconsole.log(expedition.leader.camp)', out: '2', note: 'An object inside an object.' },
      { code: 'console.log([{ a: { b: [1, 2, { c: 3 }] } }])', out: '[ { a: { b: [Array] } } ]', note: 'Node hides deep levels as [Array]. The data is still there.' },
    ],
    uses: ['API responses: a list of products, each with a price and a list of images.', 'Database results: rows (objects) in a list (array).', 'Settings files with sections inside sections.'],
    when: ['Your data has a natural hierarchy: a team has climbers, a climber has gear.', 'You have many records with the same shape. Use an array of objects.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t nest deeper than you need. `data.a.b.c.d.e` is fragile and hard to read; flatten where you can.', 'Don\'t assume every level exists in data from outside (users, the internet). Check, or use `?.`.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'const team = [{ name: "Ana" }]\nconsole.log(team[3].name)', result: 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \'name\')', why: 'There\'s no item at index 3. The message names the property it tried to read ("name") on something that was undefined.' },
      { wrong: 'team.name   // meant the first climber\'s name', result: 'undefined', why: 'team is the array, and arrays have no name property. Go one level deeper: `team[0].name`.' },
      { wrong: 'const e = { leader: {} }\nconsole.log(e.leader.tent.brand)', result: 'TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \'brand\')', why: 'e.leader.tent is missing. Use `e.leader.tent?.brand` if it might not exist.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Lost in nested data? `console.log` one level at a time: `team`, then `team[0]`, then `team[0].gear`, until you see where the shape differs from what you expected.', 'To print every level in full, `console.log(JSON.stringify(data, null, 2))` lays it all out (you\'ll learn JSON properly in Camp II).'],
    related: ['object', 'array', 'property-access', 'map-filter'],
  },
  'map-filter': {
    term: 'map and filter', short: 'map turns each item into something new; filter keeps only the items that pass a test. Both return a new array.',
    what: '`map` and `filter` are array methods that take a **function** and use it on every item:\n• **map** transforms: "give me each climber\'s name" turns a list of climber objects into a list of names. Same number of items out as in.\n• **filter** selects: "give me only the heavy items" keeps the items where your function says true. Same items, possibly fewer.\nNeither changes the original array. Both hand back a brand-new one.',
    how: 'You pass a function (usually a short arrow function) that receives each item:\n```\nconst names = team.map(c => c.name)\nconst heavy = gear.filter(item => item.kg > 5)\n```\n• **map** calls your function once per item and collects whatever it **returns** into a new array, in the same order.\n• **filter** calls your function once per item and keeps the item if the function returns something truthy (usually `true`).\n• The function can also take a second parameter, the index: `map((item, i) => …)`.\n• Because both return arrays, you can **chain** them: `gear.filter(i => i.kg > 5).map(i => i.name)` means "names of the heavy items."\n\nTheir sibling **`find`** works like filter but returns only the **first** matching item itself (not an array), or `undefined` if nothing matches. Use it when you want one thing.\n\nThe function you pass must **return** a value. With an arrow function\'s short body that happens automatically; add braces and you must write `return`.',
    usage: [
      { code: 'console.log([1, 2, 3].map(n => n * 10))', out: '[ 10, 20, 30 ]', note: 'Each item transformed.' },
      { code: 'console.log([4, 9, 2, 7].filter(n => n > 5))', out: '[ 9, 7 ]', note: 'Only the items that pass the test.' },
      { code: 'const gear = [\n  { name: "tent", kg: 6 },\n  { name: "rope", kg: 4 },\n  { name: "oxygen", kg: 7 },\n]\nconst heavy = gear.filter(i => i.kg > 5).map(i => i.name)\nconsole.log(heavy.join(", "))\nconsole.log(gear.length)', out: 'tent, oxygen\n3', note: 'Chained: filter first, then map. The original still has all 3.' },
      { code: 'const found = gear.find(i => i.kg > 5)\nconsole.log(found.name)\nconsole.log(gear.find(i => i.kg > 50))', out: 'tent\nundefined', note: 'find: the first match only, or undefined.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Turning raw data into what the screen needs: a list of products into a list of product names.', 'Search and filter features: "show only unpacked items", "only climbers above Camp II".', 'Building lists of web page elements from data (you\'ll do exactly this in React).'],
    when: ['map: you want a new array with one result per item.', 'filter: you want a subset of the items.', 'find: you want the first single item that matches.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t use map just to loop and print. If you aren\'t using the returned array, `for...of` says what you mean.', 'Don\'t use filter when you want one item. You\'d get an array back; use find.', 'For very simple "add it all up" jobs, a for...of loop with a total is often clearest while you\'re learning.'],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'console.log([1, 2].map(n => { n * 2 }))', result: '[ undefined, undefined ]', why: 'Braces mean a block body, which needs return. Write `n => n * 2` or `n => { return n * 2 }`.' },
      { wrong: 'console.log([1, 2].filter(n => { n > 1 }))', result: '[]', why: 'Same trap: no return means undefined, which counts as false, so nothing is kept.' },
      { wrong: 'const tent = gear.filter(i => i.name === "tent")\nconsole.log(tent.kg)', result: 'undefined', why: 'filter always returns an array, even for one match. Use find to get the item itself, or take `[0]`.' },
      { prose: 'Expecting map or filter to change the original array.', why: 'They never do. Store the result: `const names = team.map(c => c.name)`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Read the arrow aloud: `team.filter(c => c.camp >= 2)` is "the team, keeping each climber whose camp is at least 2."', 'Build chains one step at a time, logging after each step, before joining them up.'],
    related: ['arrow-function', 'array-methods', 'nested-data', 'for-of'],
  },
}
